# The build-probe artifacts are evidence, and their bytes are the claim.
#
# MANIFEST.sha256 records a SHA-256 per file, and the control that checks it
# compares those digests against the bytes a checkout actually produced. That
# only means anything if the bytes are the same everywhere. With no rule here,
# core.autocrlf decides per file whether it is text: the .out, .props and .xml
# files were normalised to LF on commit while the recorded digests were taken
# over their CRLF working-tree form, so six of the fifteen failed their digest
# on every non-Windows checkout -- run 38180531789 reported G1.out.txt and
# stopped there, because the control fails on the first mismatch.
#
# -text stops git from translating line endings under this directory at all.
# The manifest keeps the digests it already has, and a checkout on any platform
# now produces exactly the bytes they were taken from. This preserves the
# recorded evidence; it does not redefine what the evidence is.
build-probe/** -text

# The fork patch artefacts are evidence in the same way, and the identity of
# each one is literally its bytes: fork-exception.json records a patch_sha256
# and audit_fork_integrity.py compares it against the file on disk. Same
# failure, same shape. With no rule here, core.autocrlf=true rewrote them to
# CRLF on checkout, so a fresh clone on Windows produced 1589 bytes where the
# blob had 1540, and the digest became a4065a60... instead of the recorded
# 76389727458c..., failing both the shipped digest control and the F3 patch
# identity check. Linux CI never saw it, because autocrlf does not apply there.
#
# The artefacts are also git diff output: a context line for an empty source
# line is a single space, and the files are canonicalised without a trailing
# newline. That is why `git diff --check` reports trailing whitespace and a
# blank line at EOF here. Those are the format, not defects. Do not let
# anything "fix" them -- rewriting the bytes would invalidate the digest that
# is the whole point of storing them.
fork-provenance/** -text

# The fork-baseline fixture is evidence for the same reason: the control that
# uses it pins a SHA-256, and the declared patch is applied to those bytes. A
# CRLF rewrite on checkout would change the pre-image and the patch would stop
# applying, which reads as "the artefact is broken" rather than "the checkout
# translated the file". Same failure as above, one directory over.
tests/fixtures/fork-baseline/** -text